# Waits in Playwright

## What are Waits?

Waits are used to handle delays in web applications.

Modern websites take time to:

- Load pages
- Display elements
- Fetch API data
- Render UI components

If Playwright performs an action before the application is ready, the test may fail.

Therefore, we use waits.

--------------------------------------------------

## Why Are Waits Needed?

Example:

Step 1:
Click Login button

Step 2:
Dashboard page opens

Problem:

Dashboard may take 2-3 seconds to load.

If Playwright immediately checks for dashboard elements, the test may fail.

Waits help synchronize the test with the application.

--------------------------------------------------

# Types of Waits in Playwright

1. Auto Waiting
2. Explicit Waiting
3. Hard Wait (Not Recommended)

--------------------------------------------------

# 1. Auto Waiting (Most Important)

One of Playwright's biggest advantages.

Playwright automatically waits before performing actions.

Example:

await page.locator('#login-button').click();

Playwright automatically waits for:

- Element to be present
- Element to be visible
- Element to be enabled
- Element to be stable

Then it performs the click.

--------------------------------------------------

Without Auto Waiting

Other tools may require:

wait until button appears
wait until button is clickable
then click

--------------------------------------------------

With Playwright

Simply:

await page.locator('#login-button').click();

Playwright handles waiting internally.

--------------------------------------------------

## Auto Waiting Works For

click()

fill()

check()

uncheck()

selectOption()

hover()

press()

and most user actions

--------------------------------------------------

Example

await page.locator('#user-name')
    .fill('standard_user');

Playwright waits until textbox is ready.

--------------------------------------------------

# Actionability Checks

Before performing an action, Playwright checks:

1. Element is attached to DOM
2. Element is visible
3. Element is enabled
4. Element is stable
5. Element can receive events

Only then does the action happen.

--------------------------------------------------

# 2. Explicit Waits

Used when Auto Waiting is not enough.

We manually tell Playwright to wait for something.

--------------------------------------------------

# waitForSelector()

Waits until an element appears.

Syntax:

await page.waitForSelector('selector');

Example:

await page.waitForSelector('.inventory_item');

Useful when waiting for dynamically loaded elements.

--------------------------------------------------

# waitForURL()

Waits until URL changes.

Syntax:

await page.waitForURL('expected-url');

Example:

await page.waitForURL(
'https://www.saucedemo.com/inventory.html'
);

--------------------------------------------------

Login Example

await page.locator('#login-button').click();

await page.waitForURL(
'https://www.saucedemo.com/inventory.html'
);

--------------------------------------------------

# waitForLoadState()

Waits for page loading state.

Syntax:

await page.waitForLoadState();

Default:

await page.waitForLoadState('load');

--------------------------------------------------

Load States

1. load
2. domcontentloaded
3. networkidle

--------------------------------------------------

load

Waits until page completely loads.

Example:

await page.waitForLoadState('load');

--------------------------------------------------

domcontentloaded

Waits until HTML is loaded.

Images and CSS may still load later.

Example:

await page.waitForLoadState('domcontentloaded');

--------------------------------------------------

networkidle

Waits until no network requests are active.

Example:

await page.waitForLoadState('networkidle');

Useful after API-heavy pages.

--------------------------------------------------

# waitForResponse()

Waits for a specific API response.

Very useful in modern applications.

Syntax:

await page.waitForResponse(
response =>
response.url().includes('/users')
&& response.status() === 200
);

--------------------------------------------------

Example

await page.waitForResponse(
response =>
response.url().includes('/inventory')
);

Useful when UI depends on API data.

--------------------------------------------------

# waitForTimeout()

Hard Wait

Syntax:

await page.waitForTimeout(5000);

Meaning:

Wait exactly 5 seconds.

--------------------------------------------------

Example

await page.waitForTimeout(3000);

Playwright pauses for 3 seconds.

--------------------------------------------------

Why Hard Wait is Bad?

Suppose page loads in 1 second.

You still wait:

3 seconds

Wasting time.

--------------------------------------------------

Suppose page loads in 6 seconds.

You waited:

5 seconds

Test still fails.

--------------------------------------------------

Therefore:

Avoid hard waits whenever possible.

--------------------------------------------------

Preferred Approach

Bad:

await page.waitForTimeout(5000);

Good:

await page.waitForSelector('.inventory_item');

--------------------------------------------------

# Assertions as Waits

Playwright assertions automatically wait.

Example:

await expect(
page.locator('[data-test="title"]')
).toBeVisible();

Playwright keeps checking until:

- Element becomes visible
or
- Timeout occurs

--------------------------------------------------

Another Example

await expect(page)
.toHaveURL(
'https://www.saucedemo.com/inventory.html'
);

Playwright automatically waits for URL change.

--------------------------------------------------

# Default Timeout

Playwright default timeout:

30 seconds

If condition is not met within timeout:

Test fails.

--------------------------------------------------

Custom Timeout

await expect(
page.locator('.success')
).toBeVisible({
timeout:10000
});

Meaning:

Wait up to 10 seconds.

--------------------------------------------------

# Real Login Example

await page.goto(
'https://www.saucedemo.com'
);

await page.locator('#user-name')
.fill('standard_user');

await page.locator('#password')
.fill('secret_sauce');

await page.locator('#login-button')
.click();

await page.waitForURL(
'https://www.saucedemo.com/inventory.html'
);

await expect(
page.locator('[data-test="title"]')
).toHaveText('Products');

--------------------------------------------------

Interview Questions

Q. What are waits?

Waits help synchronize tests with the application and handle delays in loading elements, pages, and API responses.

--------------------------------------------------

Q. What is Auto Waiting?

Auto Waiting is Playwright's built-in feature that automatically waits for elements to become ready before performing actions.

--------------------------------------------------

Q. Why is Playwright famous for Auto Waiting?

Because it automatically waits for:

- Visible
- Enabled
- Stable
- Attached elements

before interacting with them.

--------------------------------------------------

Q. What is Explicit Wait?

A wait manually added by the tester.

Examples:

waitForSelector()

waitForURL()

waitForResponse()

waitForLoadState()

--------------------------------------------------

Q. Why should we avoid waitForTimeout()?

Because:

- Slows down execution
- Makes tests unstable
- Not dynamic
- Considered bad practice

--------------------------------------------------

Q. Which wait is most commonly used?

Auto Waiting

and

Playwright Assertions

--------------------------------------------------

